56,3,,status,Kenji,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":2,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
57,3,,status,Minako,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":3,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
58,3,,status,Benjamin,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":4,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
59,3,,status,Minato,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":5,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
60,3,1,talk,Kenji,,"Given the clay risk and phasing limits, a plan that minimizes deep excavation in the centre is best.","{""words"":18,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
61,3,1,talk,Benjamin,,"The drainage issues and heat readings strongly suggest we need water management and shade, not more pavement.","{""words"":17,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
62,3,1,talk,Minato,,"With only seven staff, we need a plan that volunteers can easily help with, or that requires less intensive care.","{""words"":20,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
63,3,1,talk,Minako,,"The failure of previous hard surfaces and tree deaths means we must prioritize robust, low-maintenance planting.","{""words"":16,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
64,3,1,talk,Jonathan,,"The habitat fund covers wetland restoration. While upkeep is high, the capital cost is lower for us.","{""words"":17,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
65,3,2,talk,Kenji,,"@Benjamin, does the wetland plan address the drainage callouts? It seems to reshape the water flow.","{""words"":16,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
66,3,2,talk,Benjamin,,"@Kenji, yes. It turns the problem into a feature. It manages the water instead of fighting it.","{""words"":17,""mentions"":[""Kenji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
67,3,2,talk,Minato,,"@Minako, will the wetland be easy for the volunteer groups to maintain? I worry about the complexity.","{""words"":17,""mentions"":[""Minako""],""raw_differs"":false}"
68,3,2,talk,Minako,,"@Minato, habitat tasks are already done by volunteers upriver. This fits their existing skills and interest.","{""words"":16,""mentions"":[""Minato""],""raw_differs"":false}"
69,3,2,talk,Jonathan,,"@Minako, the fund covers the water management. The upkeep is high, but it is a known, budgeted cost.","{""words"":18,""mentions"":[""Minako""],""raw_differs"":false}"
70,3,3,talk,Kenji,,"The market plaza needs hard surfacing, which the fund won't cover. That is a huge gap.","{""words"":16,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
71,3,3,talk,Benjamin,,The sports terraces also need buildings and hard surfaces. The fund exclusion makes them very expensive.,"{""words"":16,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
72,3,3,talk,Minato,,"The woodland walk is nice, but the eastern avenue record worries me. Will the trees survive?","{""words"":16,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
73,3,3,talk,Minako,,"@Minato, if we contract aftercare properly, the trees can survive. The wetland is more resilient anyway.","{""words"":16,""mentions"":[""Minato""],""raw_differs"":false}"
74,3,3,talk,Jonathan,,The wetland plan aligns with the fund's terms. It is the most financially viable option for the capital phase.,"{""words"":19,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
75,3,4,talk,Kenji,,I am leaning towards the wetland restoration. It solves the drainage and uses the fund well.,"{""words"":16,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
76,3,4,talk,Benjamin,,I agree. It addresses the heat and drainage issues directly. It is the logical choice.,"{""words"":15,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
77,3,4,talk,Minato,,"I am a bit unsure, but the volunteer support for habitat tasks makes it feel safer for me.","{""words"":18,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
78,3,4,talk,Minako,,"I vote for the wetland. It is robust, funded, and addresses the core environmental issues.","{""words"":15,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
79,3,4,talk,Jonathan,,I also vote for the wetland. It is the only plan that fits the funding terms and long-term sustainability.,"{""words"":19,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
